INT. ROOFTOP GREENHOUSE, LAUNDROMAT BUILDING - DUSK

Cracked glass panes. Tomato vines grown wild. A rusted fan turns without wind. Laundry lines sag with sheets, still dripping.

MIRIAM STOLZ-AGYEMAN (60s, silver bun, gardening gloves) trims a vine. Her son WENDELL (30s, work boots, tie loosened) sits on an upturned bucket. His wife TOVA (30s, pregnant, hand on her belly) leans in the doorway. Their neighbor, old CASPIAN DRUMMOND (80s, oxygen tank, tiny sunhat), dozes in a lawn chair.

MIRIAM
Landlord says we have until Friday.

WENDELL
Mom.

MIRIAM
Friday. That's four days.

TOVA
We'll find another roof.

MIRIAM
Another roof won't have these.

She holds up a tomato, ripe and enormous. Wendell looks at it, then at his wife.

CASPIAN
(eyes closed)
Beefsteak.

MIRIAM
Cranberry heirloom.

CASPIAN
Beefsteak.

Miriam sighs and places the tomato in Tova's hand. Tova weighs it, smiling.

TOVA
It's heavy.

WENDELL
Mom, you can't carry this up the stairs forever.

MIRIAM
Watch me.
